#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Stop hook: refuse to end a turn whose response cites a tracked org
# :ID: without footnoting it.
#
# TODO.org :ID: 2758f3a0. Measured over one session's own transcript:
# 37 of 85 citations footnoted, 43%. The failure has a precise shape --
# 25 of 32 messages footnoted every id or none, so nothing is dropped
# mid-paragraph; and every offender was footnoted correctly the FIRST
# time it appeared and dropped on re-mention. Once an id has been looked
# up it stops reading as opaque to the writer, who is holding the title.
# But the rule is per RESPONSE, and the reader of the twentieth message
# does not have the fourth one on screen.
#
# Reads `last_assistant_message', not `transcript_path'. The hook docs
# are explicit that the transcript is written asynchronously and may lag
# the in-memory conversation, so a hook needing the current turn's final
# text must use this field. Reading the transcript would check the
# PREVIOUS response and block on the wrong thing.
#
# Candidate ids are validated against the org files before being
# required. An 8-hex token is not necessarily an :ID: -- a git short
# hash is the obvious collision, and this project's prose is full of
# them -- so only tokens that actually appear as `:ID:  <token>...' in a
# tracked file are enforced. That turns a heuristic into a lookup and
# makes a false positive nearly impossible.
#
# LOOP GUARD, and it is not optional: the docs describe no
# `stop_hook_active' flag for a blocking Stop hook, so a hook that
# blocks on a condition the model then fails to satisfy blocks forever.
# A per-session sentinel, on the model of bin/hooks/block-start's,
# written when it blocks and cleared by bin/hooks/session-resume when the
# next prompt starts a new turn.
#
# CONTENT-AWARE since 2026-09-16 (TODO.org :ID: 45dc171f). It used to
# fire at most once per turn: the sentinel's mere existence silenced
# every later Stop in the same turn. That put the blind window in the
# worst possible place -- the response right after a footnote block is
# the one REWRITING footnote-heavy prose, so it is where a new miss is
# most likely, and it was exactly where the check was off. Observed
# costing two unfootnoted ids in one response.
#
# The sentinel now records the DEMAND -- the missing ids and orphaned
# markers it asked about. An identical demand is never repeated, which
# is the anti-loop property and the only one that matters: the hook can
# never ask twice for the same thing. A DIFFERENT demand blocks again,
# which is the case above. A hard bound of three blocks per turn stands
# behind that, so even a pathological alternation terminates.

set -uo pipefail

input="$(cat)"
command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || exit 0

session_id="$(jq -r '.session_id // empty' <<<"$input" 2>/dev/null)"
[[ -n "$session_id" ]] || exit 0
case "$session_id" in
  *[!A-Za-z0-9._-]*|''|.|..) exit 0 ;;
esac

queue_dir="${CLAUDE_ORG_QUEUE_DIR:-$HOME/.claude/org-updates}"
sentinel="$queue_dir/$session_id.footnote-blocked"
# Prior demands this turn, one per line. Read rather than merely tested
# for existence -- see LOOP GUARD above.
prior=""
[[ -e "$sentinel" ]] && prior="$(cat "$sentinel" 2>/dev/null)"
# Hard bound, whatever the content: three asks in one turn is enough.
if [[ -n "$prior" ]] && [[ "$(grep -c . <<<"$prior")" -ge 3 ]]; then
  exit 0
fi

message="$(jq -r '.last_assistant_message // empty' <<<"$input" 2>/dev/null)"
[[ -n "$message" ]] || exit 0

repo="${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR:-$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/../.." && pwd)}"
org_files=()
for f in "$repo/TODO.org" "$repo/DONE.org"; do
  [[ -r "$f" ]] && org_files+=("$f")
done
[[ ${#org_files[@]} -gt 0 ]] || exit 0

# Split at the LAST line that is exactly `---': everything after it is
# the footnote block. A response with no such line has no footnotes, so
# every citation in it is unfootnoted.
#
# FENCED CODE BLOCKS ARE NOT PROSE, and are dropped from the body before
# the scan. An id inside ``` or ~~~ is a *transcript* -- tool output, a
# file excerpt, a rendered slice checklist -- not a citation, and the
# footnote convention exists to keep prose scannable while staying
# recoverable.
#
# The reason is PROVENANCE and SELF-DESCRIPTION, never how a fence looks.
# Corrected 2026-09-16 (TODO.org :ID: 762b7836): an earlier wording said
# "a transcript is already in its own context", which is a claim about
# rendering -- and fences are far less prominent in the CLI than in the
# desktop app, so that argument fails exactly where the reading happens.
# What holds in any client is that the id arrived by transcription rather
# than by anyone choosing to cite it, and that a block like a slice
# checklist already carries the keyword and title a footnote would
# restate. Do not extend this exemption on the visual argument.
#
# Found 2026-08-26 by this hook firing on a response that displayed a
# slice's checklist: twelve ids, each demanding a footnote that restated
# a keyword and title already visible three lines above it inside the
# block. The convention was making the response worse, which is the
# signal that the rule was over-applying rather than that the writer was
# lazy. It got sharper the same hour: :ID: 8183fc7c made `[blocked]' name
# its ids, so pasting a whole-file outline would now demand footnotes for
# 44 ids across 17 headings.
#
# Inline code is deliberately still scanned. A backticked id in a
# sentence IS a citation -- that is the normal way one gets written --
# and exempting it would gut the check.
#
# An unclosed fence swallows the rest of the body. That is the safe
# direction: it under-reports rather than demanding footnotes for
# something the reader is looking at anyway.
body="$(awk '
  BEGIN { n = 0 }
  { l[NR] = $0; if ($0 == "---") n = NR }
  END {
    if (n == 0) n = NR + 1
    fence = 0
    for (i = 1; i < n; i++) {
      if (l[i] ~ /^[ \t]*(```|~~~)/) { fence = !fence; continue }
      if (!fence) print l[i]
    }
  }' <<<"$message")"
foot="$(awk 'BEGIN{n=0} {l[NR]=$0; if ($0=="---") n=NR} END{if(n>0) for(i=n+1;i<=NR;i++) print l[i]}' <<<"$message")"

# Candidates: 8 hex chars, deduplicated. Deliberately NOT filtered by
# shape -- the `:ID:' lookup below is the discriminator, and any shape
# test in front of it can only subtract.
#
# There WAS an `| grep -vE '^[0-9]{8}$'' here, excluding all-digit
# tokens on the reasoning that "a bare number or date is not an id".
# True of a date, false of an id: 5 of this repo's 267 tracked ids are
# all digits (29439196, 32272061, 42808717, 49557477, 51591672), and
# were therefore unenforceable. Measured 2026-08-26 after the hook let
# an unfootnoted 29439196 through, and -- worse -- blocked on a response
# citing 29439196 AND 4f6a6bb1 while naming only the second. A block
# that reports a strict subset of what is missing is the bad failure
# here: it reads as authoritative, so complying with it exactly still
# leaves the response wrong.
#
# Removing the filter costs nothing, because the case it was written for
# is already covered: a date like 20260824 is rejected by the lookup for
# not being a tracked id, which is the same test every hex token gets.
# The only way to false-positive now is prose containing an 8-digit
# number that IS one of those five -- no likelier than the hex
# collision the design already accepts.
#
# bash 3.2 throughout, which macOS still ships and which
# `#!/usr/bin/env bash' resolves to. No `mapfile', no associative
# arrays, and no bare "${arr[@]}" on a possibly-empty array under
# `set -u'. Written that way after this script's first draft used
# `mapfile' and failed every case that should have blocked -- the same
# three bash-4 constructs, in the same order, that TODO.org :ID:
# 7e9aa4ea already recorded hitting.
candidates="$(grep -oE '\b[0-9a-f]{8}\b' <<<"$body" | sort -u)"
[[ -n "$candidates" ]] || exit 0

# Resolve a tracked id to `KEYWORD<TAB>TITLE', or print nothing when the
# id is not a tracked heading. This is the lookup the script already did
# and threw away: `grep -qE' reports existence and discards the location,
# which is one awk from the heading above it -- the very keyword and
# title the message below then demands be "looked up rather than
# recalled" (TODO.org :ID: 33c3eabc).
#
# `FNR == 1' resets the remembered heading per file, or an id in the
# second file could inherit a heading left over from the end of the first.
#
# Anchored on `^:ID:' -- a property line -- rather than on the bare id,
# so an id quoted in some other heading's body cannot be mistaken for its
# own definition. That is the entry-boundary trap the org-dev skill
# records, and the anchor is what avoids it without needing Emacs.
resolve_id () { # resolve_id <id> -> "KEYWORD<TAB>TITLE"
  awk -v id="$1" '
    FNR == 1 { heading = "" }
    /^\*+ / { heading = $0 }
    # No end anchor: candidates are 8-character PREFIXES while the :ID:
    # line carries the full uuid, so anchoring the tail matches nothing.
    # Same shape as the grep this replaced. (No apostrophes in here: the
    # whole program is inside a shell single-quote.)
    $0 ~ "^:ID:[ \t]+" id {
      line = heading
      sub(/^\*+[ \t]+/, "", line)
      kw = "-"
      if (match(line, /^(TODO|NEXT|DOING|REVIEW|WAITING|MAYBE|DONE|CANCELLED)[ \t]+/)) {
        kw = substr(line, 1, RLENGTH)
        sub(/[ \t]+$/, "", kw)
        line = substr(line, RLENGTH + 1)
      }
      sub(/[ \t]+:[A-Za-z0-9_@#%:]+:[ \t]*$/, "", line)
      printf "%s\t%s\n", kw, line
      exit
    }
  ' "${org_files[@]}"
}

missing=""
resolved=""
while IFS= read -r id; do
  [[ -n "$id" ]] || continue
  found="$(resolve_id "$id")"
  [[ -n "$found" ]] || continue                         # not a tracked heading
  grep -qF "$id" <<<"$foot" && continue                 # already footnoted
  kw="${found%%$'\t'*}"
  title="${found#*$'\t'}"
  # DISCHARGED IN PLACE: a body line carrying the id AND its exact title
  # has already met the convention's purpose -- the prose stays readable
  # and the reference stays recoverable -- so a separate end-matter entry
  # would be pure duplication. This is exactly a status summary, and it
  # is where this hook was loudest: a twelve-id unwind printed all twelve
  # inline and was asked for all twelve again (TODO.org :ID: 2a6a1355).
  #
  # Same LINE, deliberately. An id in one paragraph and the title in
  # another is the ordinary unfootnoted case: a reader scanning for the
  # id does not find the title beside it, which is the whole guarantee.
  # `-e', not a bare argument: a title beginning with `-' is read as an
  # option otherwise, and grep prints a usage message straight into the
  # user's terminal. Fixed in the test harness the same morning and
  # reintroduced here within hours -- the corpus has titles starting
  # `--transcript-longer-sibling', `--session-alias-cache' and
  # `--review-format-annotation'.
  grep -F -e "$id" <<<"$body" | grep -qF -e "$title" && continue
  missing="$missing${missing:+ }$id"
  # `id` FIRST: the id is the key the prose already carries, so it is what
  # the eye scans for. The keyword is padded to a column because it is the
  # volatile field and misalignment hides staleness. Backticked because the
  # terminal renders code in a distinct face, which is the whole reason the
  # id is findable at a glance (TODO.org :ID: 2a6a1355).
  # FLUSH LEFT: the message says "append these lines", so whatever indent
  # is emitted is what gets copied, and the canonical form this hook
  # enforces has none (TODO.org :ID: cfdfe119).
  resolved="$resolved\`$id\`  $(printf '%-9s' "$kw") $title
"
done <<<"$candidates"

[[ -n "$missing" ]] || exit 0

# `mkdir -p' first: a bare `: > "$sentinel" 2>/dev/null' does NOT
# suppress the failure when the directory is absent, because bash
# processes the output redirection before the stderr one -- so the error
# still prints and, far worse, the sentinel is never written. That
# sentinel is the loop guard this script's own header calls not
# optional, and without it a Stop hook that blocks on a condition the
# model cannot satisfy blocks forever. Found 2026-08-26 by running the
# hook against a queue directory that did not exist.
# The demand this block makes, compared against the ones already made
# this turn. Identical means we have asked and the model did not satisfy
# it -- asking again is the loop the guard exists to prevent.
demand="$missing"
if [[ -n "$prior" ]] && grep -qxF -- "$demand" <<<"$prior"; then
  exit 0
fi

mkdir -p "$queue_dir" 2>/dev/null
printf '%s\n' "$demand" >> "$sentinel" 2>/dev/null

# COUNTED (TODO.org :ID: 63713df3): the sentinel above is a loop guard,
# not a record -- it says "at least once" and is cleared or kept for
# reasons unrelated to counting. One `miss' line per block is the record.
# A slim payload, never "$input": queue-append embeds what it is given,
# and the response text has no business in the queue. Fire-and-forget,
# after the guard is armed and before the block, so neither depends on it.
jq -c '{session_id, cwd, hook_event_name}' <<<"$input" 2>/dev/null \
  | "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/queue-append" miss footnote "$missing" 2>/dev/null

# STDERR AND EXIT 2. Not JSON, and not exit 0 (TODO.org :ID: 5787bfc6).
#
# The docs are explicit and this took two wrong attempts to read
# properly: "Stderr from a hook that exits 0 goes to the debug log only,
# never the transcript, and Claude never sees it." So while this script
# exited 0, NOTHING it said could reach the model in any field --
# `continue: false' blocked the turn but the message channel was shut.
# Six blocks arrived as the bare harness default, "Stop hook prevented
# continuation", and rearranging fields inside a closed channel could
# never have helped.
#
# Attempt one blamed `stopReason', which IS wrong -- it is an enum of
# "user_request"/"max_turns"/"model_stop_token"/"completion" and
# discards prose -- but fixing it changed nothing. Attempt two moved the
# text to `systemMessage' and was falsified by deliberately tripping the
# hook: the block still arrived empty. Only then did the exit code turn
# out to be the whole story.
#
# Exit 2 is the documented blocking path, and for `Stop' specifically
# the stderr text is fed back to Claude as the reason. No JSON is
# printed: "the blocking message is the reason from your JSON's blocking
# decision when it makes one, and your stderr text otherwise" -- so
# emitting no decision leaves stderr as the message, which is the whole
# point here.
cat >&2 <<EOF
Unfootnoted tracked :ID:s: $missing. Footnoting is per response. Append these
lines to the end matter, after a \`---\` separator:

$resolved
EOF
exit 2
